APACHE KAFKA — Distributed event streaming platform.

ARCHITECTURE:
Producer: writes messages to topics.
Topic: logical channel. Divided into partitions.
Partition: ordered, immutable sequence of records. Stored on disk. Replicated.
Broker: Kafka server. Hosts partitions.
Consumer group: set of consumers each reading different partitions.
 One consumer per partition within a group (parallelism = partition count).
Offset: position in partition. Consumers track their own offset.
 Committed offset: where consumer will resume after restart.
Zookeeper/KRaft: cluster coordination (Kafka 3.x moving to KRaft, no Zookeeper).

WHY KAFKA IS FAST:
Sequential disk writes: OS page cache + sequential IO = SSD/HDD near memory speed.
Zero-copy: sendfile() syscall — data moves from page cache to network without copying to userspace.
Batching: producers batch messages, consumers fetch in batches.
Compression: GZIP/Snappy/LZ4 per batch.
Retention: messages kept for configured period (default 7 days), not deleted on consume.
 Multiple consumers read same data independently. Rewind at any time.

PARTITIONING STRATEGY:
No key: round-robin across partitions.
With key: hash(key) % partitions. Same key always same partition = ordering per key.
 Order events by order_id: same order''s events always in order within partition.
Custom partitioner: override for specific routing logic.

REPLICATION:
replication.factor=3: each partition has 1 leader + 2 followers.
Leader handles all reads/writes. Followers replicate asynchronously.
ISR (In-Sync Replicas): followers within configurable lag of leader.
acks=all: producer waits for all ISR to acknowledge. Strongest durability. Slower.
acks=1: only leader acknowledges. Faster, risk of data loss on leader failure.

CONSUMER GROUPS AND LAG:
consumer_group_lag = latest_offset - committed_offset per partition.
High lag = consumer falling behind. Alert if lag > threshold.
Monitor with: kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group my-group

