COLLABORATIVE EDITING — the hardest distributed systems problem in consumer apps.

THE PROBLEM:
Two users edit the same document simultaneously. Offline edits later merged.
Must: converge to same state, preserve user intent, handle conflicts.

APPROACHES:

OPERATIONAL TRANSFORMATION (OT) — Google Docs, Firepad:
Every edit = operation: insert("hello", position=5) or delete(position=5, length=3).
When concurrent ops arrive: transform against each other.
transform(op1, op2) -> op1'' that accounts for op2 having already happened.
 User A: insert("X", pos=5). User B: insert("Y", pos=5) simultaneously.
 Server receives B first. Transforms A''s op: insert("X", pos=6). Now consistent.
Complex: O(n²) transformation cases. Requires central server as arbiter.

CONFLICT-FREE REPLICATED DATA TYPE (CRDT) — Figma, Linear:
Data structure that merges automatically with no conflicts.
Every character gets a globally unique ID. Order determined by ID.
Insert always wins. Delete = mark as "tombstone" (soft delete).
No central server needed. Peers sync directly.
Local-first: work offline, sync when connected.

Types:
G-Counter: only increments. Merge = take max per peer.
LWW-Register: Last Write Wins. Each value has timestamp, higher wins.
RGA (Replicated Growable Array): for text. Each character has unique position identifier.
YATA (Yet Another Transformation Approach): used by Yjs library.

